A||||3||mrfixit58||Cleaning Lace Wheels||||5||
Z||000000||mrfixit58||07-18-2000||10:05 PM||||The wheels on my new (used) GT look like they were only sponged off for the last 10 years. Can anyone recommend a good cleaner to break loose all those years of baked on grime and brake dust? Also, what tooks do you use to reach into the spokes and tiny spaces?<P>Thanks,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||07-18-2000||10:16 PM||||I took the wheels off the car and used some phosphoric acid containing tire cleaning solution.  Spray it on wait, use a test tube brush for the lace wheels and "Q" tips.  Rinse.  Use "Gunk" to clean the grease off the inside of the wheels, then SOS pad on the inside. Rinse.  Then the tire cleaning solution.  Don't let it set too long on the wheels.  Wear rubber gloves.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||07-18-2000||10:43 PM||||I have always had good luck using "Speedy"- like Castrol SuperClean- and the pressure washer to blast the stuff out of the tight places. Warning: Wear eye protection because hot, soapy water mixed with brake dust is no fun!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||07-19-2000||02:52 AM||||To clean the hard brake dust stained painted wheels I used "Dupli-color grease and wax remover" with a nylon "wheel brush". It cleans them up very well with minimal elbow grease to get the tough stains out. This won't harm the finish or the tires.||||reg||1
